0200"><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sbref0200"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name name-style="western"><surname>Yiu</surname><given-names>S.H.</given-names></name><name name-style="western"><surname>Wood</surname><given-names>P.J.</given-names></name><name name-style="western"><surname>Weisz</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effects of cooking on starch and β-glucan of rolled oats</article-title><source>Cereal Chemistry</source><volume>64</volume><issue>6</issue><year>1987</year><fpage>373</fpage><lpage>379</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib0205"><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sbref0205"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name name-style="western"><surname>Yiu</surname><given-names>S.H.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effects of processing and cooking on the structural and microchemical composition of oats</article-title><source>Food Structure</source><volume>5</volume><year>1986</year><fpage>219</fpage><lpage>225</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib0210"><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sbref0210"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name name-style="western"><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name name-style="western"><surname>Liang</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name name-style="western"><surname>Pei</surname><given-names>Y.</given-names></name><name name-style="western"><surname>Gao</surname><given-names>W.</given-names></name><name name-style="western"><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>Z.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effect of process on physicochemical properties of oat bran soluble dietary fiber</article-title><source>Journal of Food Science</source><volume>74</volume><issue>8</issue><year>2009</year><fpage>C628</fpage><lpage>636</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19799659</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi" assigning-authority="pmc">10.1111/j.1750-3841.2009.01324.x</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list><sec id="sec0095" sec-type="supplementary-material"><label>Appendix A</label><title>Supplementary data</title><p>The following is Supplementary data to this article:<supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="upi0005" position="float" orientation="portrait"><media xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="mmc1.docx" position="float" orientation="portrait"><?suppdata-name mmc1.docx?><?suppdata-size 2083561?><?suppdata-md5 3422165c0fbf1f751698a6662f69640e?><?suppdata-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?suppdata-mime-type application?><?suppdata-mime-sub-type vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document?><?suppdata-cloudpmc-urn urn:app:a4e5/5388193/3422165c0fbf/mmc1.docx?></media></supplementary-material></p></sec><ack id="ack0005"><title>Acknowledgements</title><p>This work was funded by the BBSRC DRINC project BB/L025272/1 and the Research Council of Norway (Project No. 225347/F40) with funds from the Norwegian Research Levy on Agricultural Products. The authors thank Dr Simon Penson (Campden BRI, Chipping Campden, UK) for useful discussions and help with the nutritional analysis, and Hanne Zobel (Nofima, Ås, Norway) for her skilful technical assistance.</p></ack><fn-group><fn id="sec0090" fn-type="supplementary-material"><label>Appendix A</label><p>Supplementary data associated with this article can be found, in the online version, at <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="doi" xlink:href="10.1016/j.carbpol.2017.02.077" id="intr0005">http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.carbpol.2017.02.077</ext-link>.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-group><fig id="fig0005" position="float" orientation="portrait"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Oat materials used in the study: flakes (A), flour (B) and BG32 (C). Oat flakes are in the mm size range. Average particle size of flour: 60 μm and oat bran BG32: 152 μm.</p></caption><alt-text id="at0005">Fig. 1</alt-text><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="gr1.jpg"><?image-name gr1.jpg?><?image-size 48592?><?image-md5 1a120cbe9a8cd58041d0ac881a239c9e?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 743?><?image-original-width 2083?><?image-scaled-height 248?><?image-scaled-width 694?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/a4e5/5388193/1a120cbe9a8c/gr1.jpg?><?thumb-name gr1.gif?><?thumb-size 11987?><?thumb-md5 1e8a721f8a02d0f99450315483afdbbb?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 71?><?thumb-scaled-width 200?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/a4e5/5388193/1e8a721f8a02/gr1.gif?></graphic></fig><fig id="fig0010" position="float" orientation="portrait"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Dissolution kinetic curves of β-glucan released from raw and hydrothermally processed (cooked) oat bran BG32 (red line), oat flour (green line) and oat flakes (blue line) containing either 0.5% (a and c) or 1.0% (b and d) of β-glucan. The data are presented as percentages of the β-glucan originally present in the oat material. The numbers in colour represent the 72 h incubation values, corresponding to the three oat samples. For presentational purposes, the dissolution data were fitted by non-linear regression using Sigmaplot. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the web version of this article.)</p></caption><alt-text id="at0010">Fig. 2</alt-text><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="gr2.jpg"><?image-name gr2.jpg?><?image-size 107979?><?image-md5 535f71975046792008b3024962008545?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 3160?><?image-original-width 3333?><?image-scaled-height 702?><?image-scaled-width 740?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/a4e5/5388193/535f71975046/gr2.jpg?><?thumb-name gr2.gif?><?thumb-size 3574?><?thumb-md5 d2a18c33ae8a3feb1f4fc911de48877b?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 95?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/a4e5/5388193/d2a18c33ae8a/gr2.gif?></graphic></fig><fig id="fig0015" position="float" orientation="portrait"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>Log-log plot of steady shear viscosity versus shear rate for guar galactomannan and β-glucan (BG1 and BG2) solutions at a concentration of 1.0% (w/v) of polymer.</p></caption><alt-text id="at0015">Fig. 3</alt-text><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="gr3.jpg"><?image-name gr3.jpg?><?image-size 39988?><?image-md5 483fd77ac628e92f84535c78f799c653?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 1299?><?image-original-width 2083?><?image-scaled-height 433?><?image-scaled-width 694?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/a4e5/5388193/483fd77ac628/gr3.jpg?><?thumb-name gr3.gif?><?thumb-size 2510?><?thumb-md5 4b6a79fafc8926b0481b2ed09a52f6bb?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 80?><?thumb-scaled-width 128?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/a4e5/5388193/4b6a79fafc89/gr3.gif?></graphic></fig><fig id="fig0020" position="float" orientation="portrait"><label>Fig. 4</label><caption><p>Log-log plot of steady shear viscosity versus shear rate obtained from the β-glucan release experiments (supernatant only) for raw (a) and cooked (b) BG32, oat flakes and oat flour after 72 h of incubation.</p></caption><alt-text id="at0020">Fig. 4</alt-text><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="gr4.jpg"><?image-name gr4.jpg?><?image-size 63214?><?image-md5 65a567ddf31b0f236f04ee13edb8bb1a?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 2285?><?image-original-width 2083?><?image-scaled-height 761?><?image-scaled-width 694?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/a4e5/5388193/65a567ddf31b/gr4.jpg?><?thumb-name gr4.gif?><?thumb-siz